  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1258 AM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nFor thursday through the weekend and into early next week, the\nOzarks will remain under the influence of upper level\nnorthwesterly flow on the eastern periphery of a strong ridge of\nhigh press over the Four Corners region. This will provide for a\npotentially active period of weather with daily shortwaves, \nfronts/boundaries and a fight between the cooler Canadian air\nmasses and the heat of the southwestern Ridge. \n\nAs a result of this pattern, there will also be occasional \nchances for thunderstorms as impulses rotate around the ridge.\nThis will be impacted however, by limited moisture flowing \nnorth through the Plains. Latest NBM PoP's remain quite low\n(20-40 percent or less) through the extended forecast. This\nkeeps better confidence that some storms will be around but \ntiming and location uncertainty is keeping the PoP lower.\n\nConfidence remains good for cooler conditions Thursday into \nFriday with highs around 90. For the weekend, temperatures \nshould see a slow climb back into the middle and upper 90s to\nstart next week. This will all depend of weather we stay more \nunder the influence of the cooler air masses or the hotter air \nfrom the ridge noses. If the warmer air makes its way into the \nregion then we will see temperatures approach 100 degrees again.\nCurrent NBM forecast is a blend which does reflect a rather \nlarge gradient, hotter in the west and cooler in the east.",
